\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Notation}
\title{One notation for the series}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{The notes use one notation. A familiar symbol is read by 5 rules,
and the rules come before any operator is fixed.}

\p{Where geometric calculus has a counterpart, that style comes first.
Mathematical physics supplies the rest, including cochains,
chains and the boundary. A familiar symbol means its finite counterpart
over a commutative ring. A continuum reading returns only as a limit,
and a geometric reading only on a recovery site. An operator is a
marked macro, not a variable letter. Each symbol has one meaning in
the series. A familiar symbol may be reused when the meaning here
agrees with it or extends it, and that reuse is explained where the
symbol is first defined.}

\p{The exterior derivative on cochains is #{{\covwedge}}, and it is the
same operator as #{{\extd}}. The contraction #{{\covdot}} is the
lowering operator given by the Clifford action of the sum of a module
and its dual, using the derived quadratic form. It is stated only where
that form exists. Elsewhere only #{{\covwedge=\extd}} is used. Their
sum is #{{\cov}}, with no sign.}

\p{The counting adjoint is not a second primitive. Under a positive
inner product,
##{\extdag=-\covdot}
The sign is the point at which the adjoint convention leaves the
algebra, and that departure is stated where the adjoint is first defined.
#{{\extD=\extd+\extdag}}. This operator is kept when a statement needs
a self-adjoint operator, and the relation to #{{\cov}} is cited there.
A statement that does not need self-adjointness is written with
#{{\cov}}.}

\p{A square is written #{{\covsq}}. The positive operator #{\Delta} is
the counterpart #{-\covsq}, and that letter appears only with this
correspondence. The weighted operator #{L=-\extdag W\extd} is that
weighted square, and the identification is stated with the definition
of #{L}. #{\partial} is only the boundary of a chain. A difference
along a direction #{a} is #{a\cdot\cov}. The small delta is only the
Kronecker delta, including the function #{\delta_x} given by
#{\delta_x(y)=\delta_{xy}}.}
